  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe primary approach is to employ a derivative-free optimization method, specifically the COBYLA algorithm, which is better suited for non-convex problems. Additionally, we use a multistart strategy by perturbing the initial guess multiple times to explore different regions of the search space. This combination aims to escape local minima and potentially find a lower value for C\u2085.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Decision variables: h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] for i = 1, ..., n_points\n- Objective: Minimize t where t \u2265 max_k [dx * \u03a3 h[i](1 - h[i + k])]\n- Constraints:\n  - \u03a3 h[i] = n_points / 2\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- The problem is non-convex due to the quadratic terms h[i]h[i + k].\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize with initial_h_values\nb. Generate perturbed initial guesses:\n   - For each start in 1..n_starts:\n      i. Add random noise to h_initial\n      ii. Clip to [0, 1]\n      iii. Rescale to ensure \u03a3h = n_points / 2\nc. For each perturbed h:\n   i. Run COBYLA optimizer\n   ii. Track best h and corresponding C\u2085\nd. Return best solution found\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- n_starts = 5\n- COBYLA settings: maxiter=800, rhobeg=0.1, rhoend=1e-5\n- Perturbation scale: 0.1 * sqrt(1 / n_points)\n- Rescale after perturbation using sum constraint\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each optimization: ~200s\n- Total: 5 optimizations \u2248 1000s, within budget\n- Robust against local minima via multiple starts\n- Feasibility maintained via clipping and rescaling\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure \u03a3h = n_points / 2 and 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Verify C\u2085 using evaluator\n- Return best h found before time limit",
